<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Deliberately almost empty.

  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays YES, so Xcode merges everything it already
  generates (display name, health usage strings, orientations, scene manifest)
  into this file at build time. Only the keys Xcode CANNOT express as an
  INFOPLIST_KEY_ build setting live here — INAlternativeAppNames is an array of
  dictionaries and there is no build-setting shape for that.

  Keeping it minimal is the point: a hand-written full Info.plist would be a
  second place for the health usage strings to live, and a missing usage string
  is an upload rejection rather than a warning.

  It sits BESIDE the Wrought folder rather than inside it, on purpose. That
  folder is a synchronized root group, so anything dropped in gets target
  membership automatically — which is exactly what makes adding a Swift file
  free, and exactly what would make this file get copied into the bundle as an
  ordinary resource while ALSO being the app's real Info.plist. Two Info.plists
  in one bundle is a build failure with a misleading message.

  These nicknames are what let somebody say "hey Siri, gym bro" and reach this
  app. Every AppShortcut phrase must contain the application name, so the way to
  make the real sentence work is to teach Siri that the app answers to the name
  people actually use for it. "Jim Bro" is here because that is what dictation
  makes of "gym bro" roughly half the time — the same reason the connector's
  phrasebook carries it.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>INAlternativeAppNames</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Gym Bro</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Jim Bro</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Broski</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Wrought</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Coach</string>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
